1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,342
A coronary angiogram uses four tools that
do different jobs.

2
00:00:04,586 --> 00:00:06,745
The sheath gives the team a way in.

3
00:00:06,954 --> 00:00:09,195
The guidewire helps direct equipment.

4
00:00:09,439 --> 00:00:13,549
The catheter carries contrast, and the
contrast makes the heart’s arteries show

5
00:00:13,607 --> 00:00:14,582
up on X-ray.

6
00:00:14,826 --> 00:00:18,135
The team can enter an artery at the wrist
or the groin.

7
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:20,642
We’ll follow a right wrist example.

8
00:00:21,246 --> 00:00:25,937
The catheter travels through connected
arteries to the aorta, the large artery

9
00:00:26,006 --> 00:00:27,295
that leaves the heart.

10
00:00:27,899 --> 00:00:31,045
The groin route reaches the same area from
below.

11
00:00:31,741 --> 00:00:35,770
At the numb wrist, the team places a fine
needle into the artery.

12
00:00:36,211 --> 00:00:40,240
In this example, a short access wire
passes through the needle.

13
00:00:40,681 --> 00:00:43,862
The needle comes out and the wire stays in
place.

14
00:00:44,466 --> 00:00:48,111
That short wire helps the team put the
sheath into the artery.

15
00:00:48,622 --> 00:00:51,165
The sheath is a short, hollow tube.

16
00:00:51,548 --> 00:00:54,857
It goes in with a tapered insert that
helps it enter.

17
00:00:55,553 --> 00:00:59,710
The insert and short wire come out,
leaving the sheath at the wrist.

18
00:01:00,406 --> 00:01:05,514
It gives the team a passage to move a
catheter in or out without making another

19
00:01:05,572 --> 00:01:06,594
puncture there.

20
00:01:07,198 --> 00:01:12,248
At the groin, the team may use a finer
wire and a temporary tube before

21
00:01:12,318 --> 00:01:13,897
placing the working sheath.

22
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:16,706
The steps and equipment can vary.

23
00:01:17,217 --> 00:01:21,907
Once the working sheath is in place, it
serves the same purpose as at the wrist.

24
00:01:22,604 --> 00:01:26,098
Back at the wrist, a longer guidewire goes
through the sheath.

25
00:01:26,702 --> 00:01:31,799
Live X-ray, called fluoroscopy, helps the
cardiologist see it travel through the

26
00:01:31,857 --> 00:01:36,965
arteries. The thin, flexible diagnostic
catheter slides over that wire

27
00:01:37,058 --> 00:01:39,403
toward the aorta near the heart.

28
00:01:40,216 --> 00:01:42,062
The sheath stays at the wrist.

29
00:01:42,503 --> 00:01:47,449
Near the heart, the cardiologist withdraws
the long guidewire from the catheter.

30
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:50,003
The catheter stays in place.

31
00:01:50,607 --> 00:01:55,901
The team positions its curved tip at the
opening of a coronary artery, just above

32
00:01:55,948 --> 00:02:00,394
the aortic valve. The wire is out before
the contrast

33
00:02:00,441 --> 00:02:01,393
injection.

34
00:02:01,904 --> 00:02:06,617
The team injects a liquid called contrast
through the hollow catheter.

35
00:02:07,314 --> 00:02:12,271
As it mixes with blood and moves through
the coronary artery, it makes the channel

36
00:02:12,388 --> 00:02:15,011
inside the artery visible on X-ray.

37
00:02:15,615 --> 00:02:19,261
The outline fades as the contrast moves on
with the blood.

38
00:02:19,864 --> 00:02:25,077
The team records a short series of X-rays
while contrast is flowing, so the pictures

39
00:02:25,124 --> 00:02:26,796
show the arteries filling.

40
00:02:27,179 --> 00:02:30,488
They can move the X-ray camera for another
view.

41
00:02:31,091 --> 00:02:36,049
They position the catheter at the other
coronary opening for a separate injection

42
00:02:36,327 --> 00:02:37,976
and set of pictures.

43
00:02:38,673 --> 00:02:43,758
Before the test, tell the team about
kidney problems or any past reaction to

44
00:02:43,874 --> 00:02:48,692
X-ray contrast. The contrast usually
contains iodine.

45
00:02:49,203 --> 00:02:52,303
Some people feel briefly warm during an
injection.

46
00:02:52,814 --> 00:02:57,493
If you feel unwell, especially if you have
chest discomfort, itching or trouble

47
00:02:57,528 --> 00:02:59,873
breathing, tell the team right away.

48
00:03:00,477 --> 00:03:04,761
Once the test is finished, the team
removes the catheter and sheath and checks

49
00:03:04,819 --> 00:03:09,533
the entry site. The sheath provided a
passage, the guidewire guided the

50
00:03:09,579 --> 00:03:12,783
catheter, and the catheter delivered the
contrast.

51
00:03:13,387 --> 00:03:17,091
The moving X-rays are what the
cardiologist reviews next.
